The '''flag of the [[Bolodun and Kuvari Islands]]''' is a red field with a black fourteen-pointed star in the centre bearing a yellow circle at its core. The flag was adopted following the country's independence in 1926 and reflects the socialist character of the state and the union of the island federation's two principal ethnic communities.

== Design ==

The flag consists of a red field, a black fourteen-pointed star centred upon it, and a small yellow circle at the centre of the star. The fourteen points of the star each represent one of the fourteen inhabited islands of the country, while the centre of the star is occupied by the yellow circle.

== Symbolism ==

The red field symbolises the global movement for socialism, reflecting the Kadarist character of the state established after independence. The black star represents the fourteen main islands of the federation; its black colour honours the [[Sudanese Kaftians of the Bolodun and Kuvari Islands|Sudanese Kaftian]] people, the country's largest ethnic community. The yellow circle in the centre of the star represents the [[Kuvari people]], the indigenous population of the islands, placed at the heart of the design as the federation's historic people.

== History ==

The flag was adopted in the aftermath of independence in 1926, when the [[Socialist Party of Bolodun]] formed the first government of the new state. Its design was chosen to express both the socialist identity of the republic and the shared sovereignty of the islands' two principal communities. It has remained the national flag since that time.

